    <!-- PLAIN SUMMARY -->
    <div class="sec" style="border-top:none;">
      <div class="plain-summary">
        <strong>Hezbollah in plain language:</strong> Hezbollah is a Lebanese Shia organization that is simultaneously a political party with seats in parliament, a paramilitary force larger than the Lebanese Army, and a provider of schools and hospitals in Lebanon's south. Created by <a href="/iran">Iran</a>'s <span class="term" data-def="Islamic Revolutionary Guard Corps. Iran's parallel military force that answers only to the Supreme Leader. Created Hezbollah in 1982 and continues to fund, arm, and direct it.">IRGC</span> in 1982 following Israel's invasion of Lebanon. 27 countries designate it a <span class="term" data-def="Foreign Terrorist Organization. A designation issued by the U.S. Secretary of State under INA §219. Triggers criminal liability for providing material support and financial sanctions.">terrorist organization</span>. It fought Israel in 2006 and again in 2024. Its longtime leader Hassan Nasrallah was killed by Israel on September 27, 2024. The organization has been severely degraded but is actively reorganizing under new Secretary-General Naim Qassem. Hezbollah's founding 1985 Open Letter called explicitly for an Islamic state in Lebanon on the <span class="term" data-def="The political ideology of Ayatollah Ruhollah Khomeini. Centers on Velayat-e Faqih (clerical supreme rule), anti-Americanism, anti-Zionism, and the establishment of Islamic governance. The model Iran exported to Hezbollah in 1982.">Khomeinist</span> model and used dehumanizing language toward Jews. On non-Muslim Lebanese neighbors — chiefly the country's Christian communities — the document offered conditional coexistence: those who didn't align with "imperialism and Zionism" could remain, but under an Islamic system rather than the existing multi-confessional republic. Christian militias aligned with Israel (primarily the Phalangists) were named enemies. In a 2009 manifesto Hezbollah dropped the demand for an Islamic state and softened its rhetoric, acknowledging Lebanon's confessional reality — but, as with <a href="/org/hamas">Hamas</a>'s 2017 document, the 1985 Open Letter was never formally repealed.
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- LEADERSHIP STATUS -->
    <div class="sec">
      <div class="sec-ey">Leadership · As of June 2026</div>
      <h2 class="sec-headline">Three Leaders in Seven Days — Then Qassem</h2>
      <p class="sec-lead">In the span of a week in late September and early October 2024, Israel killed Hassan Nasrallah (32 years as Secretary-General) and then Hashem Safieddine (Nasrallah's successor, dead 2 days after taking office). Naim Qassem, deputy secretary-general since 1991, was elected by the <span class="term" data-def="Hezbollah's supreme governing body. A secretive council of senior figures that makes strategic decisions and elects the Secretary-General. Its composition is not publicly disclosed.">Shura Council</span> on October 29, 2024. He faces the dual challenge of rebuilding a shattered command structure while managing an internal power struggle between pragmatic and hardline factions.</p>

      <div class="status-panel">
        <span class="sp-icon">⚠️</span>
        <span class="sp-text">As of March 2026, Lebanon's Minister of Justice is considering legal action against Naim Qassem. Wafiq Safa — head of Hezbollah's Coordination and Liaison Unit since 1987 and one of the most senior figures in the organization — resigned in February 2026. Hezbollah is in active post-war restructuring, shifting from centralized command to decentralized cells to reduce vulnerability to targeted strikes.</span>
      </div>

      <div class="leader-grid">
        <div class="leader-card">
          <div class="lc-avatar" style="background:#4a3800;">NQ</div>
          <div class="lc-name">Naim Qassem</div>
          <div class="lc-role">Secretary-General · since Oct 29, 2024</div>
          <span class="lc-badge lb-current">Incumbent</span>
          <div class="lc-note">Born Feb 1953. Deputy Secretary-General for 33 years (1991–2024). Elected by Shura Council to succeed Nasrallah. Backed by Iran in consolidating power. Faces prosecution threat from Lebanese government (Mar 2026).</div>
        </div>
        <div class="leader-card">
          <div class="lc-avatar" style="background:#555;">HN</div>
          <div class="lc-name">Hassan Nasrallah 🕯</div>
          <div class="lc-role">Secretary-General · 1992–2024</div>
          <span class="lc-badge lb-dead">Assassinated Sep 27, 2024</span>
          <div class="lc-note">Led Hezbollah for 32 years. Killed in Israeli strike on Hezbollah HQ in Dahieh, Beirut. Widely regarded as one of the most consequential figures in the modern Middle East.</div>
        </div>
        <div class="leader-card">
          <div class="lc-avatar" style="background:#555;">HS</div>
          <div class="lc-name">Hashem Safieddine 🕯</div>
          <div class="lc-role">Secretary-General · Oct 1–3, 2024</div>
          <span class="lc-badge lb-dead">Assassinated Oct 3, 2024</span>
          <div class="lc-note">Nasrallah's cousin and head of Executive Council since 2001. Assumed office October 1; killed by Israeli airstrike on Dahieh October 3 — 2 days in office.</div>
        </div>
